35
00:03:27,120 --> 00:03:29,760
So, first of all, what is WebRTC?

36
00:03:29,920 --> 00:03:42,560
So, as I have it in the definition here, WebRTC is a set of JavaScript APIs that allows us to establish a peer-to-peer connection between two browsers to exchange data such as audio and video, all in real time.

37
00:03:42,670 --> 00:03:44,452
So, this is real-time communication.

38
00:03:44,974 --> 00:03:54,000
So the thing that makes WebRTC so special is the fact that the connection is between two browsers and the data that transmits between the browsers never actually reaches a server.

39
00:03:54,080 --> 00:03:56,484
So it doesn't mean that we don't need a server involved at all.

40
00:03:56,814 --> 00:04:04,960
But as this picture shows right here, let's say we have two clients, that data is transmitted directly once a connection is made and never has to reach that server.

41
00:04:05,040 --> 00:04:14,532
So this makes WebRTC ideal for exchanging audio and video because any latency that would be added by having to hit the server first would actually cause a slight delay.

42
00:04:15,278 --> 00:04:20,068
So I know some of you are thinking if it's real-time communication, this sounds a lot like WebSockets.

43
00:04:20,462 --> 00:04:23,392
So WebSockets and WebRTC are similar in a lot of aspects.

44
00:04:23,472 --> 00:04:30,272
So I want to break down what WebSockets are and how that functions, and then we'll make some comparisons on when you might want to use one over the other.

45
00:04:30,512 --> 00:04:34,032
So WebSockets, the connection is peer-to-server right here.

46
00:04:34,126 --> 00:04:39,712
So we have a browser to server connection versus WebRTC where the connection is browser to browser.

47
00:04:39,872 --> 00:04:42,272
So one is peer-to-peer, one is peer-to-server.

48
00:04:42,352 --> 00:04:44,804
So they both allow us to communicate in real time.

49
00:04:45,134 --> 00:04:49,312
But with WebSockets, let's say peer one right here wanted to send a chat message to peer two.

50
00:04:49,838 --> 00:04:50,752
They would send the message.

51
00:04:50,832 --> 00:04:53,392
That message would go to the server and then out to peer two.

52
00:04:53,632 --> 00:04:59,172
Now if peer two wants to respond, they would send that message to the server and then out to peer one.

53
00:04:59,630 --> 00:05:11,268
So there's a little bit of latency with WebSockets and having to first go to the server, but it happens so fast that half a second or maybe a second wouldn't really make much of a difference for something like a chat message or a notification.

54
00:05:12,014 --> 00:05:17,472
But if we wanted to send audio and video data through WebSockets, that latency would be very noticeable.

55
00:05:17,552 --> 00:05:23,972
I'm sure we've all been on a call or seen a video where the two people are talking, there's a little bit of latency and they're talking over each other.

56
00:05:24,270 --> 00:05:26,672
So that creates a pretty big issue right there.

57
00:05:26,752 --> 00:05:28,512
So this is where WebRTC thrives.

58
00:05:28,592 --> 00:05:37,892
So by not having to reach the server first, the connection is directly between the two browsers and that video data can be exchanged or audio data can be exchanged much faster.

59
00:05:38,318 --> 00:05:47,108
Now with that, WebRTC uses UDP as its protocol to transport the data, and UDP is very fast, and more on that in a second.

60
00:05:47,982 --> 00:05:56,032
So if WebRTC is so fast, why would we need WebSockets if they both give us real-time communication and one can transfer data faster for us?

61
00:05:56,112 --> 00:05:59,072
It seems like WebRTC is the optimal path here.

62
00:05:59,374 --> 00:06:02,592
Any more speed in our application always seems like a better thing here.

63
00:06:02,672 --> 00:06:04,992
So there's some limitations to WebRTC.

64
00:06:05,072 --> 00:06:08,592
So let's talk about this and then when you might want to use one over the other.

65
00:06:08,752 --> 00:06:15,940
So first of all, WebRTC uses UDP, and UDP is not a reliable protocol for transferring important data.

66
00:06:16,366 --> 00:06:22,512
So the way UDP works is that it sends data really fast, but it never validates if the data was received.

67
00:06:22,672 --> 00:06:30,592
So because of that, if we're sending something like an audio or video data, if we lose a couple of frames from our video, that's no big deal, it'll catch up.

68
00:06:30,672 --> 00:06:35,652
Your video might look a little bit funny for a second, but everything will turn out and it's not a big issue.

69
00:06:36,142 --> 00:06:42,832
If we're sending something like a file to someone, if we lose a couple of bytes of data from that file, that entire file can be corrupted.

70
00:06:42,992 --> 00:06:45,472
So because of that, UDP is not reliable.

71
00:06:45,552 --> 00:06:47,844
You would not want to use it for sending important data.

72
00:06:48,238 --> 00:06:51,632
Now, also with WebRTC, there is no built-in signaling.

73
00:06:51,712 --> 00:06:55,472
So this means that you can't just use WebRTC and make a connection.

74
00:06:55,822 --> 00:07:02,992
WebRTC actually leaves it up to us to make that initial connection, and then once a connection is made, then WebRTC takes over.

75
00:07:03,232 --> 00:07:07,472
So this is where WebSockets and WebRTC actually go good together.

76
00:07:07,726 --> 00:07:10,756
We would use a process called signaling with WebSockets.

77
00:07:11,086 --> 00:07:16,112
We would send the information between the two peers, the connections made, and then WebRTC takes over.

78
00:07:16,192 --> 00:07:18,564
So WebRTC has its limitations in that way.

79
00:07:19,566 --> 00:07:20,832
So how does all of this work?

80
00:07:20,912 --> 00:07:25,152
So I'm gonna give you a quick example first, and then we'll actually start breaking down the details of this.

81
00:07:25,232 --> 00:07:33,392
But at a high-level overview, we have two peers, and if they want to communicate, we would have one peer initiate a connection and they would say, Hey, you want to chat?

82
00:07:33,472 --> 00:07:35,712
And they would have to send this message out to peer two.

83
00:07:36,078 --> 00:07:40,112
So they would send out a message, it doesn't matter how it's done, this can be done through signaling.

84
00:07:40,302 --> 00:07:44,004
They can send that message through an email, a tweet, it's irrelevant.

85
00:07:44,174 --> 00:07:47,552
But that message would contain some information about this peer right here.

86
00:07:47,694 --> 00:07:50,148
So they would have to send their network information to peer two.

87
00:07:50,798 --> 00:07:56,192
Now, if peer two accepts this offer, they would go ahead and send some information back to peer one.

88
00:07:56,526 --> 00:08:07,552
The means of communication right now is not relevant, but the second that information is exchanged, data can begin to flow, and now the two peers do not need a server involved, they can start transmitting data.

89
00:08:07,694 --> 00:08:11,172
So this can be audio and video data, and that connection is made.

90
00:08:12,302 --> 00:08:16,880
So, what exactly are we sending between the two peers and how are we sending this data?

91
00:08:17,040 --> 00:08:21,840
So, as I mentioned earlier, that means of communication in the beginning is actually irrelevant.

92
00:08:21,920 --> 00:08:26,880
That could be a tweet or an email, but usually this is through a process called signaling.

93
00:08:26,960 --> 00:08:29,700
That's a more practical way of putting this into an application.

94
00:08:30,094 --> 00:08:34,240
And that can be through WebSockets or some kind of third-party signaling service.

95
00:08:34,350 --> 00:08:35,200
It doesn't really matter.

96
00:08:35,360 --> 00:08:39,200
So you'd get the two peers in some kind of room so they can at least know about each other.

97
00:08:39,440 --> 00:08:41,360
Then they would transmit the initial data.

98
00:08:41,440 --> 00:08:49,120
Now, in that data, there are two main components: there's a session description protocol, an SDP, and some ICE candidates.

99
00:08:49,280 --> 00:08:56,800
So, first of all, both peers will need to exchange a session description protocol, one in the form of an offer, one in the form of an answer.

100
00:08:57,134 --> 00:09:05,600
And a session description protocol is simply an object containing some kind of information about both peers, usually like the codec, address, media type, audio, and video.

101
00:09:05,680 --> 00:09:08,880
So just a bunch of information about your network and how to connect to you.

102
00:09:08,960 --> 00:09:11,812
So both peers will need to know about each other's network.

103
00:09:12,238 --> 00:09:19,760
Now, along with exchanging session description protocols between the two peers, both peers will also exchange a series of ICE candidates.

104
00:09:19,840 --> 00:09:26,640
And an ICE candidate is simply a public IP address and port that could be potentially used as an address to receive data.

105
00:09:26,720 --> 00:09:35,524
So each peer will typically have multiple ICE candidates that are gathered by making a series of requests to a stun server, and they are exchanged between the two peers.

106
00:09:35,886 --> 00:09:40,420
So we'll actually get into this exchange process and we'll go more into detail about what's happening.

107
00:09:40,640 --> 00:09:44,580
So exchanging session description protocols and ice candidates.

108
00:09:45,070 --> 00:09:51,040
So right here, we have two peers right here, and we have a server, and then we also have stun servers that we're going to connect to.

109
00:09:51,120 --> 00:09:55,364
So we'll go through that process again, the one that we went through in that high-level overview.

110
00:09:55,726 --> 00:09:58,320
And the first peer will say, Hey, let's connect.

111
00:10:05,646 --> 00:10:11,440
Now peer two will receive that SDP and they can accept it and they'll say, Hey, sure, here's my SDP.

112
00:10:11,566 --> 00:10:12,068
Let's connect.

113
00:10:12,430 --> 00:10:17,200
The second that's exchanged, the two peers are connected, but data can't start flowing yet.

114
00:10:17,280 --> 00:10:18,756
They've simply made that connection.

115
00:10:19,918 --> 00:10:25,920
So before data can start flowing between the two peers, we still need to coordinate the discovery of our public IP addresses.

116
00:10:26,080 --> 00:10:30,724
So that's because most devices nowadays sit behind firewalls and NAT devices.

117
00:10:31,054 --> 00:10:33,600
So to do this, we're gonna use a method called ICE.

118
00:10:33,680 --> 00:10:40,068
So this means we're gonna make a series of requests to a stun server, get our ICE candidates, and then transfer them between the two peers.

119
00:10:40,398 --> 00:10:45,840
And a stun server is not something that you're going to have to worry about setting up, it's really cheap and easy to maintain.

120
00:10:46,190 --> 00:10:50,564
So there's a lot of public stun servers, and in our example, we'll use a Google stun server.

121
00:10:50,926 --> 00:10:52,964
So let's go ahead and continue with this process.

122
00:10:53,454 --> 00:11:02,244
So what's going to happen here is once the SDP offer and answer are exchanged, this peer right here will go ahead and ask the stun server, hey, what's my public IP address?

123
00:11:02,766 --> 00:11:10,400
Now the stun server will reply and they'll send over a series of ICE candidates to this peer, and this peer will send over the ICE candidates to peer two.

124
00:11:10,830 --> 00:11:12,240
Now, peer two will do the same.

125
00:11:12,462 --> 00:11:18,640
They're going to go ahead and make a request to the stun server and say, Hey, what's my IP address or public IP?

126
00:11:19,054 --> 00:11:22,948
Stun server will respond and the ICE candidates are exchanged.

127
00:11:23,150 --> 00:11:33,476
Now, once we find or the network finds an optimal path to communicate through with these ICE candidates, data can start flowing between the two peers and that entire connection is complete.

128
00:11:34,542 --> 00:11:38,080
Now I wanted to talk about one more thing, and that is trickling ICE candidates.

129
00:11:38,160 --> 00:11:42,628
So how you send over ICE candidates is up to you, but there's a few ways you can go about doing this.

130
00:11:42,990 --> 00:11:46,628
And let's go ahead and take a look at this process all over again, and I'll try to break this down.

131
00:11:46,800 --> 00:11:53,220
So when we first create our STP offer, we also make a series of requests to a stun server to generate our ICE candidates.

132
00:11:53,710 --> 00:12:02,692
Now, what we could do is create our STP offer, wait till all our ICE candidates are generated, and then send over the STP offer and the ICE candidates all together.

133
00:12:02,960 --> 00:12:07,268
Now, more traditionally, what you want to do is not have to wait for this process to complete.

134
00:12:07,534 --> 00:12:12,960
You want to send over your STP offer and then trickle in your ICE candidates as they're created.

135
00:12:13,326 --> 00:12:20,160
So you'll notice that we sent over the STP offer, and then we don't get back our ICE candidates in a batch, they're sent over individually.

136
00:12:20,270 --> 00:12:24,640
So we would signal over each ice candidate over to our peer as they are generated.

137
00:12:24,814 --> 00:12:30,240
So we would send over the offer, send over an ice candidate and another and another, and then that process would continue.

138
00:12:30,320 --> 00:12:36,880
So that is called trickling ice, and we're actually gonna run through this so it's gonna help us fix a little bit of a delay here.

139
00:12:37,040 --> 00:12:39,172
So let's go ahead and jump to the next slide.

