1335
01:34:43,920 --> 01:34:48,388
So at this point, our application only handles two users, so it's just peer-to-peer.

1336
01:34:48,622 --> 01:34:53,860
And group video calling is an entirely different topic in WebRTC, and there's multiple ways we can go about doing this.

1337
01:34:54,222 --> 01:35:01,188
So we're not going to build anything out, but I do want to give you a high-level overview of how to handle that before we move on to the next project in this video.

1338
01:35:01,838 --> 01:35:07,600
So, how do we take a technology that's fundamentally designed to handle peer-to-peer connections and go beyond that?

1339
01:35:07,854 --> 01:35:11,680
Well, it turns out there's actually three different approaches to solve this exact issue.

1340
01:35:11,760 --> 01:35:13,156
So let's go ahead and go over them.

1341
01:35:13,998 --> 01:35:25,572
The first approach we have is called a mesh network, and with the mesh network, we are creating a network of peers that will all maintain a peer-to-peer connection with every other peer inside of a group video call.

1342
01:35:25,760 --> 01:35:28,388
So let's say we have a call with five peers.

1343
01:35:28,686 --> 01:35:41,680
Well, peer one will have to create a peer-to-peer connection to peer two, then to peer three, four, and five, and all the other peers in the network will have to create that same connection with every other peer inside of that network.

1344
01:35:42,030 --> 01:35:46,756
So it kind of meshes them together, and this will happen for every other peer that has to join after this.

1345
01:35:47,150 --> 01:35:53,156
So every new peer that joins will have to create this connection, and all the other peers will have to connect back to this peer.

1346
01:35:53,390 --> 01:35:57,892
So every single time a new peer joins, we now have to maintain a new connection.

1347
01:35:58,160 --> 01:36:04,480
And this technically does accomplish what we need here, but this approach has a massive downside and does not scale at all.

1348
01:36:04,686 --> 01:36:10,820
The problem here is that every single time a new peer joins, we now have to upload our stream and maintain that connection.

1349
01:36:11,182 --> 01:36:13,572
So this requires a lot of upload bandwidth.

1350
01:36:13,680 --> 01:36:18,240
So once you go beyond four to five peers, you're gonna start seeing a lot of issues here.

1351
01:36:18,350 --> 01:36:21,476
So this approach is not recommended if you're looking to scale.

1352
01:36:21,966 --> 01:36:32,480
Now the second approach we're going to talk about is less of a peer-to-peer architecture and more of a peer-to-server architecture, and that is the MCU approach, which stands for multi-conferencing unit.

1353
01:36:32,640 --> 01:36:46,788
So how this works is now each peer will be connected to the server, and unlike the mesh network, we're going to upload our stream to that server, and that server will take in all the incoming streams, process them, mix them together, and then send them back to the peers.

1354
01:36:47,150 --> 01:36:52,068
Now, the upside to this is that it does scale better, and you only have to upload your stream once.

1355
01:36:52,462 --> 01:37:05,636
The downside is that that server that you're gonna send this to has to be very beefy, it has to process all of this, so it could get very expensive to actually handle this, so it scales better, but the downside is that it's gonna be more expensive.

1356
01:37:06,094 --> 01:37:21,796
For the third and final approach, we have the SFU method, which stands for selecting forwarding unit, and this is very similar to the MCU approach in the sense that we still have a peer-to-server architecture and we're still only uploading our stream once to that server.

1357
01:37:22,094 --> 01:37:28,800
Now, where this differs from the MCU approach is that we're no longer going to mix those streams together and process them.

1358
01:37:29,040 --> 01:37:37,060
The server will now act as a router and simply just make sure that we get all of our peers streams and that all of our peers get our stream.

1359
01:37:37,358 --> 01:37:39,908
So it's simply going to direct traffic and that's all it's doing.

1360
01:37:40,080 --> 01:37:49,764
So the benefit to this is that because we're no longer mixing and processing all those streams, we now don't need such a beefy server and it's going to be a lot less expensive.

1361
01:37:50,158 --> 01:37:59,360
Now, while this approach does have a lot of benefits to it, one thing to be mindful of is that we're still only uploading one stream, but we are now having to download multiple streams.

1362
01:37:59,470 --> 01:38:07,040
So if we start having a group video call with maybe 20, 30, 40 people, you do have to be mindful of people's download speed.

1363
01:38:07,246 --> 01:38:15,716
Most people nowadays would probably be fine with this, but it's just something to consider is the fact that you do need download speed in order to start downloading all those streams.

1364
01:38:16,206 --> 01:38:26,276
So the benefit that the MCU approach has is that users only need to upload once and download once with the SFU method, they upload once and then download multiple times.

1365
01:38:26,734 --> 01:38:31,200
Now, in part two of this video, we are going to be creating a group video call and application.

1366
01:38:31,406 --> 01:38:38,160
But instead of using one of the three approaches that I talked about, we're going to go another route and we're just going to use a third-party service.

1367
01:38:38,320 --> 01:38:44,036
So anytime you're building out an application like this, you have to decide do I want to build all this out by myself?

1368
01:38:44,398 --> 01:38:51,760
Do I want to set up that infrastructure and deal with scaling this application, or is there a third-party service that can make my life a lot easier?

1369
01:38:51,920 --> 01:39:03,360
So the trade-offs is that usually a third party service is going to cost you some money, but that can also save you money down the line if you're having to still pay engineers or spend time on building this out.

1370
01:39:03,520 --> 01:39:07,780
So I wanted to make sure that you can see both approaches, make that decision for yourself.

